1
00:00:15,235 --> 00:00:19,698
[SPEAKER_03]: This is in Gautama Nikaya, book of 11, sutta number 9, called Vissanda.

2
00:00:20,759 --> 00:00:23,461
[SPEAKER_03]: At one time, the Buddha was staying in Jnatika, in the brick house.

3
00:00:23,801 --> 00:00:28,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, Venerable Sanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side.

4
00:00:28,905 --> 00:00:32,848
[SPEAKER_03]: The Buddha said to him, Sanda, meditate like a thoroughbred, not like a wild colt.

5
00:00:33,448 --> 00:00:39,753
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like a thoroughbred would be a well-tamed horse, and a wild colt would be like an untamed horse.

6
00:00:40,834 --> 00:00:42,435
[SPEAKER_03]: And how does a wild colt meditate?

7
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:47,083
[SPEAKER_03]: A wild colt tied up by the feeding trough meditates.

8
00:00:47,724 --> 00:00:48,745
[SPEAKER_03]: Fodder, fodder.

9
00:00:49,285 --> 00:00:54,089
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, he's just concerned with his food, with the hay that that's given to him.

10
00:00:54,870 --> 00:00:55,410
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that?

11
00:00:55,490 --> 00:01:02,016
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it doesn't occur to the wild colt tied up by the feeding trough, what task will the horse trainer have me do today?

12
00:01:02,717 --> 00:01:03,617
[SPEAKER_03]: How should I respond?

13
00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:06,680
[SPEAKER_03]: Tied up by the feeding trough, it just meditates.

14
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,561
[SPEAKER_03]: Fodder, fodder.

15
00:01:08,688 --> 00:01:16,431
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same way, take a certain untrained person who has gone to the forest, the root of a tree, or an empty hut.

16
00:01:17,071 --> 00:01:23,734
[SPEAKER_03]: Their mind is overcome, admired in sensual desire, and they don't understand, as it is, the escape from arisen sensual desire.

17
00:01:25,055 --> 00:01:30,217
[SPEAKER_03]: Harboring sensual desire internally, they meditate and contemplate and cogitate and ruminate.

18
00:01:31,037 --> 00:01:37,460
[SPEAKER_03]: Their mind is overcome by ill will, indolence and lethargy, restlessness and anxiety, doubt.

19
00:01:38,382 --> 00:01:42,492
[SPEAKER_03]: Harboring doubt internally, they meditate and contemplate, and cogitate and ruminate.

20
00:01:43,242 --> 00:01:46,063
[SPEAKER_03]: They meditate dependent on earth, water, fire, and air.

21
00:01:46,383 --> 00:01:54,466
[SPEAKER_03]: They meditate dependent on the extent of infinite space, infinite consciousness, nothingness, or neither perception nor non-perception.

22
00:01:55,427 --> 00:01:57,928
[SPEAKER_03]: They meditate dependent on this world or the other world.

23
00:01:58,088 --> 00:02:05,571
[SPEAKER_03]: They meditate dependent on what is seen, heard, thought, cognized, attained, sought, or explored by the mental faculty.

24
00:02:06,191 --> 00:02:08,552
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how an untrained person meditates.

25
00:02:08,652 --> 00:02:10,393
[SPEAKER_03]: And how does a thoroughbred meditate?

26
00:02:11,758 --> 00:02:16,821
[SPEAKER_03]: A fine thoroughbred tied up by the feeding trough doesn't meditate, fodder, fodder.

27
00:02:17,061 --> 00:02:17,561
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that?

28
00:02:18,161 --> 00:02:21,303
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it occurs to the fine thoroughbred tied up by the feeding trough.

29
00:02:22,143 --> 00:02:24,884
[SPEAKER_03]: What task will the horse trainer have me do today?

30
00:02:24,964 --> 00:02:25,845
[SPEAKER_03]: How should I respond?

31
00:02:26,505 --> 00:02:30,547
[SPEAKER_03]: Tied up by the feeding trough, they don't meditate fodder, fodder.

32
00:02:31,027 --> 00:02:36,349
[SPEAKER_03]: For that fine thoroughbred regards the use of the goad as a debt, a bond, a loss, a misfortune.

33
00:02:37,249 --> 00:02:44,092
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same way, take a certain fine thoroughbred person who has gone to the forest, the river tree, or an empty hut.

34
00:02:44,832 --> 00:02:51,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Their mind is not overcome and mired in sensual desire, and they understand as it is the escape from arisen sensual desire.

35
00:02:52,854 --> 00:02:58,298
[SPEAKER_03]: Their mind is not overcome by a will, indolence and lethargy, restlessness and anxiety, doubt.

36
00:02:58,998 --> 00:03:01,580
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't meditate dependent on earth, water, fire and air.

37
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:08,225
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't meditate dependent on the extent of infinite space, infinite consciousness, nothingness or neither perception nor non-perception.

38
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:11,387
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't meditate dependent on this world or the other world.

39
00:03:11,467 --> 00:03:17,972
[SPEAKER_03]: They don't meditate dependent on what is seen, heard, thought, cognized, attained, sought or explored by the mental faculty.

40
00:03:18,692 --> 00:03:19,733
[SPEAKER_03]: Yet, they do meditate.

41
00:03:20,495 --> 00:03:22,415
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is basically the most important part.

42
00:03:22,455 --> 00:03:22,695
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh...

43
00:03:50,432 --> 00:03:50,913
[SPEAKER_03]: There we have it.

44
00:03:50,953 --> 00:03:57,220
[SPEAKER_03]: The Buddha is explicitly advising against any type of absorption meditation.

45
00:03:58,421 --> 00:04:03,006
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what does it mean?

46
00:04:05,008 --> 00:04:05,969
[SPEAKER_03]: We can start with...

47
00:04:08,788 --> 00:04:25,124
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no description or discussion of what mindfulness is, because oftentimes mindfulness nowadays is regarded precisely as that, as a type of absorption on one thing, or at least sort of focused attention on one thing.

48
00:04:26,385 --> 00:04:39,592
[SPEAKER_03]: Usually the breath or bodily sensations, body scanning, and even thoughts, which all of that is included by what the Sutta said, what is sensed, cognized, thought, explored by the mental faculty.

49
00:04:39,832 --> 00:04:41,073
[SPEAKER_03]: That's like literally anything.

50
00:04:41,413 --> 00:04:44,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and people think that samādhi is like absorption.

51
00:04:44,994 --> 00:04:50,735
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, in the Noble Eightfold Path, it talks about sati, mindfulness, and then samādhi.

52
00:04:51,475 --> 00:04:58,856
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, people think mindfulness is about focusing on something, which then naturally has to result in the samādhi, which is the absorption of that thing.

53
00:04:58,976 --> 00:05:04,537
[SPEAKER_03]: Fixed state, when the focusing has been consummated.

54
00:05:04,837 --> 00:05:05,097
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

55
00:05:05,857 --> 00:05:06,838
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

56
00:05:06,858 --> 00:05:09,218
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, what... let's say...

57
00:05:11,182 --> 00:05:11,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Mindfulness.

58
00:05:11,742 --> 00:05:24,130
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if it isn't about focusing, if it isn't about just looking at what's in front of you, like the wild colt is just looking at the hay that's in front of him, and instead it says the thoroughbred,

59
00:05:25,367 --> 00:05:30,430
[SPEAKER_03]: He wonders about something that's broader than what's in front of him.

60
00:05:30,911 --> 00:05:38,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he still meditates, but not on, as it says there, earth, water, fire, air, or what is being perceived, basically.

61
00:05:38,335 --> 00:05:53,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so you can see the list is... when you see these kinds of lists in the suttas, like in Majjhima Nikaya 1, where lists basically every possible permutation of anything, that means there is no

62
00:05:55,053 --> 00:06:00,035
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no object or thing that would be good for you to focus on.

63
00:06:01,075 --> 00:06:06,417
[SPEAKER_03]: The right mindfulness is the opposite direction of that, which is exactly what it says.

64
00:06:07,778 --> 00:06:15,701
[SPEAKER_03]: The horse takes a step back from what's actually going on and discerns a broader context.

65
00:06:16,041 --> 00:06:18,882
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the principle of mindfulness.

66
00:06:19,522 --> 00:06:24,084
[SPEAKER_03]: So... and I think people often don't realize that

67
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:32,789
[SPEAKER_03]: They do have mindfulness, or a certain principle of mindfulness, underlying their everyday actions, which is what allows them to function.

68
00:06:35,171 --> 00:06:44,241
[SPEAKER_03]: But nevertheless, because of the views nowadays, it gets conceived as this basically artificial, contrived practice, that nobody would do.

69
00:06:44,982 --> 00:06:47,003
[SPEAKER_03]: Unless you tell them.

70
00:06:47,563 --> 00:06:48,504
[SPEAKER_03]: Is what mindfulness is.

71
00:06:48,604 --> 00:06:53,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, sometimes, you already have mindfulness and you may not even realize it, because you think it's something else.

72
00:06:53,486 --> 00:06:54,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, maybe we should start there.

73
00:06:55,007 --> 00:06:59,869
[SPEAKER_01]: That mindfulness is not something that you fully have to create from scratch.

74
00:07:00,009 --> 00:07:00,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

75
00:07:00,589 --> 00:07:07,012
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's basically already a structural aspect of experience that needs to be understood and developed.

76
00:07:07,252 --> 00:07:07,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

77
00:07:08,172 --> 00:07:09,153
[SPEAKER_03]: So, so, eh...

78
00:07:09,433 --> 00:07:11,174
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought of giving an example.

79
00:07:11,254 --> 00:07:13,595
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you have, eh...

80
00:07:16,087 --> 00:07:28,631
[SPEAKER_03]: When you, let's say, go to a supermarket, and you know what you have to buy, it's one specific thing, and you're supposed not to spend too much time in the supermarket.

81
00:07:28,651 --> 00:07:33,653
[SPEAKER_03]: You're supposed to be... go somewhere else, and there's a certain deadline there, time constraint.

82
00:07:34,253 --> 00:07:34,513
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

83
00:07:36,509 --> 00:07:54,786
[SPEAKER_03]: There are going to be lots of distractions in the supermarket, and you would have at the back of your mind a sort of perspective of this is what I need to buy, and I need to be out of here in 15 minutes.

84
00:07:56,107 --> 00:08:00,712
[SPEAKER_03]: And even though your perceptions are occupied with

85
00:08:01,832 --> 00:08:13,940
[SPEAKER_03]: The things that you're seeing in the supermarket, which might not be the thing that you are specifically looking for, you're walking around, looking through the different aisles and whatnot, and eh...

86
00:08:15,423 --> 00:08:26,672
[SPEAKER_03]: Your perception is... like these are the fodder, like the wild horse is what's in front of you, the things that you're seeing, but at the back of your mind you have this context of why you're actually there.

87
00:08:27,572 --> 00:08:39,642
[SPEAKER_03]: And so that's what's actually allowing you to, you know, when you see, I don't know, something that you would have otherwise become distracted by, captivated by,

88
00:08:41,683 --> 00:08:45,925
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't, because you have that broader context in mind, even though you did see the perception.

89
00:08:46,385 --> 00:08:50,807
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not that you, sort of like, blinded yourself.

90
00:08:51,127 --> 00:08:58,291
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that is actually a principle of mindfulness, and you realize it's not even something you're doing.

91
00:08:59,284 --> 00:09:13,269
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, it's not not even... because if it were on the level of what you're choosing to attend to, then, immediately, when you choose to attend to the things that are in front of you, which are not what you're looking for, you will lose your mindfulness.

92
00:09:13,690 --> 00:09:14,210
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's right.

93
00:09:14,970 --> 00:09:21,457
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that already shows that these are two levels happening in your experience, and... At the same time.

94
00:09:21,557 --> 00:09:28,364
[SPEAKER_03]: At the same time, and that level where the memory is, or the context is, that it... that doesn't even require effort.

95
00:09:29,165 --> 00:09:31,848
[SPEAKER_03]: It requires a negative effort, basically, of

96
00:09:32,959 --> 00:09:50,090
[SPEAKER_03]: Not allowing yourself to, as you said, get captivated by anything specific, because if you allow that to happen, then that's when the memory will fade, and you will find, oh, 10 minutes have passed by, and I was just, you know, grabbing all this other stuff that I didn't actually need.

97
00:09:50,831 --> 00:09:58,796
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's a basic form of mindfulness that everybody would have, and you would realize some people

98
00:09:59,873 --> 00:10:06,475
[SPEAKER_03]: They have such little amount of mindfulness that they couldn't even fulfill that type of task reliably.

99
00:10:06,695 --> 00:10:16,939
[SPEAKER_01]: They would basically forget why they went to the grocery store, come back home with a bag of stuff, which also didn't contain what they went to the grocery store for to begin with.

100
00:10:18,219 --> 00:10:27,402
[SPEAKER_03]: So, eh... the focusing direction is like... there is some overlap.

101
00:10:28,556 --> 00:10:33,842
[SPEAKER_03]: With you being mindful and you being focused, but the overlap is actually quite small.

102
00:10:35,564 --> 00:10:48,738
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, yes, if you every second try to focus on the thought of what you have to get, technically, yes, you won't forget, but you're not actually understanding that the memory

103
00:10:49,727 --> 00:10:54,611
[SPEAKER_03]: Of the context of why you're in the supermarket, that actually endures at a different level of your attention.

104
00:10:55,192 --> 00:10:58,055
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're overdoing the practice.

105
00:10:58,235 --> 00:11:05,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to recollect a hundred times, when you have only 10-15 minutes in the supermarket, what it is that you want to get.

106
00:11:05,921 --> 00:11:08,364
[SPEAKER_01]: That is not how it would ordinarily work.

107
00:11:08,384 --> 00:11:08,724
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

108
00:11:09,144 --> 00:11:16,311
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's also because of these views that get imposed over the practice, which is...

109
00:11:16,941 --> 00:11:26,289
[SPEAKER_03]: It's mainly originated from the Abhidhamma and the commentaries that the mind is made up of these discrete moments that follow individually.

110
00:11:26,529 --> 00:11:29,812
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, there's... nothing endures for more than one mind moment.

111
00:11:30,012 --> 00:11:37,238
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, the view then is, every mind moment needs to have mindfulness in it, like me actively doing that mindfulness.

112
00:11:37,638 --> 00:11:38,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Because otherwise,

113
00:11:39,911 --> 00:11:40,431
[SPEAKER_03]: I will forget.

114
00:11:42,212 --> 00:11:45,273
[SPEAKER_01]: That results in a rather neurotic take on what mindfulness is.

115
00:11:45,373 --> 00:11:45,613
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

116
00:11:45,673 --> 00:11:52,135
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you would realize that type of mindfulness, you will never be able to have it always.

117
00:11:52,475 --> 00:12:00,958
[SPEAKER_03]: You will only be able to have it in periods where you're basically not functioning as a person.

118
00:12:01,258 --> 00:12:04,759
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're just fully sitting down,

119
00:12:06,127 --> 00:12:09,949
[SPEAKER_03]: Absorbed, dedicated to nothing else but the practice.

120
00:12:10,509 --> 00:12:18,033
[SPEAKER_03]: And, technically, from that point of view, every other moment of your life, as the Buddha said, someone who is not mindful is as if dead.

121
00:12:19,153 --> 00:12:29,378
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that means, like, 90% of your time, unless you're on retreat, always, you're being heedless, because you're not mindful, if that's what mindfulness is.

122
00:12:29,418 --> 00:12:29,658
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

123
00:12:30,799 --> 00:12:33,340
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, if you understand it in this way,

124
00:12:34,505 --> 00:12:59,636
[SPEAKER_03]: Which, by the way, that was obviously a mundane example of mindfulness, what I just gave the example of the supermarket, but if we now take it on a more accurate level of the mind and actually make it relevant to the freedom from suffering, you could see, well, you would have the same recollection of, let's say, not acting out of unwholesome states.

125
00:13:01,207 --> 00:13:13,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we should clear up that there's this difference between focusing on things in front of you and recollecting context, which is what the mindfulness is, as we have now established.

126
00:13:13,456 --> 00:13:18,399
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, we need to give some content, quote-unquote, to what is the right context.

127
00:13:23,253 --> 00:13:27,774
[SPEAKER_03]: The establishing of mindfulness is that you do need to attend to it for a moment.

128
00:13:27,814 --> 00:13:31,075
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you can't just expect that the context will be already there.

129
00:13:31,635 --> 00:13:35,296
[SPEAKER_03]: It's more like setting it up and that it endures on its own.

130
00:13:35,436 --> 00:13:36,476
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the same with this.

131
00:13:37,296 --> 00:13:42,458
[SPEAKER_03]: You do have to, for a moment, think about, okay, I am supposed to leave the supermarket in 15 minutes.

132
00:13:43,098 --> 00:13:45,578
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not that if you don't think about it, it will be there on its own.

133
00:13:45,598 --> 00:13:46,739
[SPEAKER_03]: But once you think about it,

134
00:13:47,899 --> 00:13:56,105
[SPEAKER_03]: It just sort of floats there on its own, and you don't need to be constantly... Now, if you find yourself getting distracted, then you need to think about it again.

135
00:13:56,286 --> 00:14:00,069
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, I'm supposed to get out of here at 9.15 or whatever.

136
00:14:00,549 --> 00:14:02,370
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then you bring it back.

137
00:14:02,811 --> 00:14:08,055
[SPEAKER_03]: But throughout the time where that context was there, that mindfulness was there, floating on its own, you don't need to be

138
00:14:09,477 --> 00:14:11,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, that would actually distract you.

139
00:14:11,377 --> 00:14:20,140
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, imagine if you're just totally going in that direction of trying to remember it every second, you won't even be able to pay attention to what's happening in front of you.

140
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:30,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so you may find actually sort of the product that you're looking for, and you can read the label, pay attention to that, trying to understand what it says, the label of the product, right?

141
00:14:30,782 --> 00:14:37,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not directly attending to that context, but that doesn't mean that you have lost your mindfulness on that occasion.

142
00:14:37,904 --> 00:14:39,264
[SPEAKER_01]: It is only when you then stray

143
00:14:40,004 --> 00:14:40,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

144
00:14:40,885 --> 00:14:44,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same way, you would have, for example, a context of...

145
00:14:44,206 --> 00:14:45,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I should not act out of an wholesome state.

146
00:14:45,247 --> 00:14:47,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I shouldn't break the precepts.

147
00:14:47,648 --> 00:14:49,408
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would go through your life,

148
00:15:07,112 --> 00:15:13,074
[SPEAKER_03]: And many times, you would have moments where you are factually like you're going to work or something.

149
00:15:13,374 --> 00:15:16,335
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't... like you have to pay attention to what you're doing.

150
00:15:16,355 --> 00:15:17,895
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't be thinking about this the whole time.

151
00:15:18,676 --> 00:15:22,237
[SPEAKER_03]: But, you can still be mindful of that.

152
00:15:22,477 --> 00:15:24,357
[SPEAKER_03]: And how would you know that you've lost your mindfulness?

153
00:15:24,377 --> 00:15:33,740
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, when you catch yourself making an intention to do something that was... that you had previously determined not to do, then you know, I lost my mindfulness.

154
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:35,961
[SPEAKER_03]: The thing is, you cannot have like a...

155
00:15:36,985 --> 00:15:47,532
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot have, like, an external point of view, where you see, like, the mindfulness enduring, and then, this is the second where it's faded.

156
00:15:48,072 --> 00:15:48,933
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not how it works.

157
00:15:49,253 --> 00:15:52,315
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that would... Then, right there, you pick it back up.

158
00:15:52,355 --> 00:15:53,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

159
00:15:53,436 --> 00:15:53,756
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

160
00:15:53,816 --> 00:15:57,919
[SPEAKER_03]: That requires, that would require you to have an external perspective to your own experience.

161
00:15:58,419 --> 00:15:59,860
[SPEAKER_03]: And mindfulness is quite the opposite.

162
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:03,203
[SPEAKER_03]: Mindfulness is, like, it comes before your point of view.

163
00:16:03,903 --> 00:16:06,145
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't, like, watch it as an object.

164
00:16:06,745 --> 00:16:12,593
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it's saying, like, the bright meditation doesn't depend even on what's explored by the mental faculty.

165
00:16:13,114 --> 00:16:17,399
[SPEAKER_03]: What's explored by the mental faculty comes second to the mindfulness, if it's established properly.

166
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:21,284
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you won't even be able to, like, watch it fading.

167
00:16:21,545 --> 00:16:22,806
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just you find yourself...

168
00:16:24,118 --> 00:16:29,321
[SPEAKER_03]: After the fact, you can't even trace, like, how long, how much time passed.

169
00:16:30,341 --> 00:16:39,986
[SPEAKER_03]: You find yourself, oh, I lost my mindfulness, because I was about to do something, break a precept, whereby, even though I had determined, I wouldn't do it.

170
00:16:40,107 --> 00:16:41,247
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you have to restart it.

171
00:16:41,727 --> 00:16:42,228
[SPEAKER_03]: But, eh...

172
00:16:42,908 --> 00:16:46,992
[SPEAKER_03]: The point is, you don't develop a view that it's about constantly having it there.

173
00:16:47,673 --> 00:16:51,737
[SPEAKER_03]: It's about restarting it or refreshing it when you lose it.

174
00:16:52,698 --> 00:17:04,770
[SPEAKER_03]: And the funny thing is, there is a certain sense of the direction of mindfulness that's embedded into a lot of the modern practices, like the noting, for example.

175
00:17:05,686 --> 00:17:13,829
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you know, when you're doing something, and then you're telling yourself, sitting, sitting, sitting, walking, walking, walking, bending, bending, bending...

176
00:17:14,149 --> 00:17:28,775
[SPEAKER_03]: The thing is, like, if you were totally oblivious to that broader context of, like, what your body is doing, and then you bring your mind back to that, that is the direction of mindfulness.

177
00:17:29,015 --> 00:17:30,155
[SPEAKER_03]: But the thing is, if you now...

178
00:17:30,616 --> 00:17:33,637
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, you established it, but now you keep attending to it every second.

179
00:17:34,623 --> 00:17:42,188
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, now you're overdoing it, because the point was not to... to... like, you actually lost the mindfulness as a result of doing that, because you became absorbed.

180
00:17:42,928 --> 00:17:59,539
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole point of the mindfulness should have been, okay, I am sitting, and now that sort of peripheral image or idea endures in your mind, while it's now... like, we can do it right now, for example, like...

181
00:18:01,579 --> 00:18:08,463
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so, I remember that I am here, sitting down, even while I'm talking, my body is in this position.

182
00:18:09,904 --> 00:18:13,106
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, now I'm thinking about other things.

183
00:18:13,827 --> 00:18:18,990
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not particularly thinking about my body sitting down, but that memory is still there, enduring.

184
00:18:19,470 --> 00:18:21,972
[SPEAKER_03]: And that, until that body changes posture, for example.

185
00:18:21,992 --> 00:18:22,292
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

186
00:18:22,913 --> 00:18:23,393
[SPEAKER_03]: So then,

187
00:18:25,083 --> 00:18:26,124
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I haven't thought about it.

188
00:18:26,444 --> 00:18:28,385
[SPEAKER_03]: I still haven't forgotten about the fact that I'm sitting.

189
00:18:29,286 --> 00:18:31,767
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, that's the principle.

190
00:18:32,868 --> 00:18:33,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there might be a...

191
00:18:34,029 --> 00:18:44,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ordinarily, when one hasn't come across the Buddha's teaching, mindfulness is already in a small degree there, as you said in the beginning, as a structural principle, but then people are not developing it.

192
00:18:45,316 --> 00:18:53,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But on account of wrong instructions and wrong ideas, those people who are developing mindfulness, there's a high chance that they might actually overdo it.

193
00:18:54,662 --> 00:18:54,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

194
00:18:55,042 --> 00:18:59,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now a similar thing occurs to me as well, just to continue with the grocery store.

195
00:18:59,764 --> 00:19:14,531
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suppose somebody has to buy something in the shop, they need to walk there, and they say, well, let's... this is the wrong type of mindfulness, but just as an illustration, and they say, let's use this 10-minute walk to the grocery store to practice mindfulness.

196
00:19:15,151 --> 00:19:20,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they pay attention to every time they put their right foot on the ground and every time they put their left foot on the ground.

197
00:19:21,314 --> 00:19:26,896
[SPEAKER_01]: And they actually pay attention every time they put their foot on the ground for the next 10 minutes.

198
00:19:26,916 --> 00:19:27,977
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't miss a single step.

199
00:19:28,437 --> 00:19:32,219
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they find that they actually walked to their friend's house and are about to ring the doorbell there.

200
00:19:33,199 --> 00:19:36,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, did that person now practice mindfulness correctly or not?

201
00:19:37,021 --> 00:19:37,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

202
00:19:37,482 --> 00:19:43,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people might say, yeah, because, you know, you were actually, you know, walking, walking, feeling every time my foot's on the ground.

203
00:19:44,450 --> 00:19:46,673
[SPEAKER_01]: I felt it there, so I was there with my mind.

204
00:19:47,154 --> 00:19:51,760
[SPEAKER_01]: But the whole point is, you were too concerned with that narrow, specific...

205
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:52,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Exercise.

206
00:19:53,421 --> 00:19:58,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to realize that you were just going to do that, because you had something more important to do.

207
00:19:58,123 --> 00:20:04,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Namely, to get that item from the grocery store, which somebody has now completely spaced out, and they ended up doing something different.

208
00:20:05,267 --> 00:20:17,994
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and also, you often sometimes even hear the view that, like, when you... Oh, I was meditating, and I totally forgot what was happening.

209
00:20:18,014 --> 00:20:20,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, my concentration was so good,

210
00:20:21,956 --> 00:20:25,718
[SPEAKER_03]: That, like, I didn't even realize that time was passing by or something.

211
00:20:25,758 --> 00:20:26,899
[SPEAKER_03]: I just totally forgot.

212
00:20:27,219 --> 00:20:28,399
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's regarded as a good thing.

213
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:32,722
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas mindfulness is actually awareness.

214
00:20:33,302 --> 00:20:34,503
[SPEAKER_03]: It's quite the opposite direction.

215
00:20:34,523 --> 00:20:38,105
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're so aware that you can't even overlook anything.

216
00:20:38,205 --> 00:20:39,245
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, but on the

217
00:20:41,011 --> 00:20:45,474
[SPEAKER_03]: On that level of your actions, your attention, the mindfulness precedes the attention.

218
00:20:45,955 --> 00:20:55,002
[SPEAKER_03]: So, essentially, it would be impossible for you to say something like, I forgot where I was, or something.

219
00:20:55,022 --> 00:21:04,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because if attention is secondary, you would have a memory, or you would be able to recollect what it is that you were attending to, if the mindfulness

220
00:21:04,989 --> 00:21:07,971
[SPEAKER_01]: is more fundamental than whatever it is that you're attending to.

221
00:21:08,151 --> 00:21:08,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

222
00:21:08,591 --> 00:21:08,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

223
00:21:09,332 --> 00:21:09,872
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

224
00:21:10,312 --> 00:21:30,425
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the fundamental purpose of mindfulness of the body, which is like the most popular one, because it does, ironically, or unfortunately, allow, or misconceiving it into a kind of absorption or focusing practice.

225
00:21:31,446 --> 00:21:34,568
[SPEAKER_03]: But how do you focus on your mental state?

226
00:21:36,040 --> 00:21:37,420
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is the third satipaṭṭhāna.

227
00:21:37,940 --> 00:21:41,761
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you focus on feeling, which are not sensations?

228
00:21:41,881 --> 00:21:42,081
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

229
00:21:42,862 --> 00:21:59,185
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you focus on... because it says there, if feelings were sensations, then why does the satipaṭṭhāna sutta says, I know when I experience a feeling of the flesh, I know when I experience a feeling that's not of the flesh, meaning it's not a sensation.

230
00:22:00,206 --> 00:22:05,607
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how can you focus on the Four Noble Truths?

231
00:22:08,128 --> 00:22:23,531
[SPEAKER_03]: So, all of these things are on that same level of a peripheral context and mindfulness of the body is exactly the same.

232
00:22:24,571 --> 00:22:25,312
[SPEAKER_03]: It's nothing special.

233
00:22:26,432 --> 00:22:32,573
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's the purpose of, for example, the mindfulness of the postures is, like I said, I mean, I've been talking the whole time.

234
00:22:32,613 --> 00:22:35,274
[SPEAKER_03]: I haven't forgotten the fact that I am sitting, even though

235
00:22:36,234 --> 00:22:38,415
[SPEAKER_03]: The mind is going in all sorts of different directions.

236
00:22:38,995 --> 00:22:49,657
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's... why... why then... why is it... why would it be beneficial to have that memory of the body there?

237
00:22:50,578 --> 00:22:59,460
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's say the posture that it's in or something like the asubha practice or breathing

238
00:23:01,124 --> 00:23:02,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Why would that be beneficial?

239
00:23:03,125 --> 00:23:24,755
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not a... now that we've clarified that it's not a focusing... Yeah, so now that we've clarified that body is not an object in front of you that you attend to, but is itself a context that one can recollect, which is one of the right recollections, so now you're asking why is that part of the actual dhamma and why is that wholesome?

240
00:23:25,175 --> 00:23:25,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

241
00:23:38,131 --> 00:23:38,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Any ideas, anyone?

242
00:23:54,410 --> 00:23:56,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

243
00:24:02,623 --> 00:24:17,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can see that this thing that is like this kind of context of being embodied, you could say, it doesn't require you're putting it there.

244
00:24:19,071 --> 00:24:26,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there whether you're kind of like attending to it or not.

245
00:24:27,475 --> 00:24:31,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you kind of keep that in the background, then

246
00:24:32,607 --> 00:24:39,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you do, whatever you see, whatever you think of throughout the day, is put into this...

247
00:24:39,335 --> 00:24:48,285
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to put this in my own words instead of just using the buzzwords, but...

248
00:24:57,561 --> 00:25:04,925
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess it can just kind of show you that it is not something that is your doing, in a way.

249
00:25:05,085 --> 00:25:08,147
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not dependent upon your putting it there.

250
00:25:08,747 --> 00:25:09,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is it?

251
00:25:09,927 --> 00:25:15,010
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens when you don't have that type of perspective?

252
00:25:16,571 --> 00:25:23,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you kind of just overlook it and take it on as, like, for granted.

253
00:25:23,294 --> 00:25:24,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, this is...

254
00:25:26,775 --> 00:25:48,581
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess you kind of put yourself there, and you don't... you don't see it as being something that is necessary for whatever you're engaging in, I guess.

255
00:25:48,661 --> 00:25:53,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for instance, if you're not kind of aware of there being a tongue,

256
00:25:56,132 --> 00:26:05,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're like eating, you might kind of act in a heedless way, just kind of like taking on that taste, for instance.

257
00:26:06,546 --> 00:26:07,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas...

258
00:26:07,827 --> 00:26:13,595
[SPEAKER_03]: So when it says, um, harboring sensual desire internally,

259
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:33,169
[SPEAKER_03]: In relation to that, harboring the hindrances internally, when a person focuses, when a person is just thinking about what's in front of them, like the wild cult, how does... actually establishing the context, like it says they're asking, the horse asking himself, what does the trainer want me to do today?

260
00:26:33,750 --> 00:26:40,173
[SPEAKER_03]: How does that fix that situation of being overcome by sensual desire, aversion, and so on?

261
00:26:42,892 --> 00:26:51,373
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's kind of taking a different... it's including that internal aspect within your kind of contemplation.

262
00:26:52,094 --> 00:26:53,954
[SPEAKER_00]: But what what happens when it's not included?

263
00:26:56,734 --> 00:27:03,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in that case, then it is internal and you're kind of... you're there, essentially.

264
00:27:03,576 --> 00:27:11,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Like it's a... you kind of become susceptible to whatever that is susceptible to.

265
00:27:12,578 --> 00:27:23,787
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so the point being, you don't need to explicitly be heedless or do anything immoral, do anything bad.

266
00:27:25,109 --> 00:27:30,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Not having mindfulness, in and of itself, opens the way for greed, aversion and delusion.

267
00:27:32,735 --> 00:27:40,822
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's... the fact of regaining that perspective over your experience,

268
00:27:42,045 --> 00:27:48,353
[SPEAKER_03]: If the perspective is properly established, any of these things, like, what is sensual desire?

269
00:27:48,793 --> 00:27:53,739
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you be sensually obsessed with an object, if you have perspective?

270
00:27:56,130 --> 00:28:00,373
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't, because sensory desire is precisely the loss of perspective.

271
00:28:00,513 --> 00:28:02,135
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not the pleasure of the object.

272
00:28:02,895 --> 00:28:04,757
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not the perception of it.

273
00:28:05,217 --> 00:28:07,739
[SPEAKER_03]: It's when you... all of these hindrances, actually.

274
00:28:08,059 --> 00:28:10,541
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not the things that arose in your mind.

275
00:28:10,681 --> 00:28:13,323
[SPEAKER_03]: It's when you become absorbed in it.

276
00:28:14,164 --> 00:28:17,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and absorption and the right mindfulness, that's a contradiction.

277
00:28:17,807 --> 00:28:18,387
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so...

278
00:28:19,408 --> 00:28:27,095
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything, then, that becomes... that you become absorbed in, automatically introduces one of the five hindrances.

279
00:28:28,396 --> 00:28:41,808
[SPEAKER_03]: Even if it's a mental thing that has nothing to do with the usual things that you perceive in the world, if it's just a mental perception during an absorption experience.

280
00:28:43,363 --> 00:28:53,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Desire, that the principle of desire, the principle of wanting, the principle of appropriation, is there for as long as you lack perspective in regard to that thing.

281
00:28:54,325 --> 00:29:05,928
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why, ironically, with this view of absorption, many people would say, in jhāna, you are attached, or you're sort of indulging in this object that has arisen.

282
00:29:06,088 --> 00:29:06,668
[SPEAKER_03]: But actually,

283
00:29:08,287 --> 00:29:10,908
[SPEAKER_03]: The peace comes from the opposite direction.

284
00:29:11,108 --> 00:29:30,137
[SPEAKER_03]: When you withdraw yourself from anything that your attention could have been taken by, while the attention is still there, you don't destroy the attention, but you simply... you basically develop this vantage point.

285
00:29:31,845 --> 00:29:33,646
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what, you could say, that's what mindfulness is.

286
00:29:33,686 --> 00:29:40,408
[SPEAKER_03]: Mindfulness is like a vantage point, and while everything else is happening here, well, that vantage point is kept.

287
00:29:40,508 --> 00:29:48,611
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it doesn't matter that even the content of what's here, you realize all the problems arose because you were here.

288
00:29:49,612 --> 00:30:00,476
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't matter what, even if it's a good thing that was down here, if you are there, there is sensual desire, aversion, restlessness, craving of some kind.

289
00:30:01,342 --> 00:30:10,672
[SPEAKER_03]: Craving for more of this pleasant experience that I think is wholesome, but because I'm absorbed in it, well, the principle of wanting more pleasure is still there, and that's what's unwholesome.

290
00:30:12,837 --> 00:30:14,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I have a question.

291
00:30:14,277 --> 00:30:25,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose somebody follows the discussion so far, and they see, okay, mindfulness is not getting lost in that direction, but it's establishing this context, or this spiritual awareness, as you said.

292
00:30:25,780 --> 00:30:31,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they try to practice that, and they say, it's too vast, it's too spacious,

293
00:30:32,162 --> 00:30:37,767
[SPEAKER_01]: To just dwell on that context, there's nothing really there for me to hold on to.

294
00:30:38,247 --> 00:30:43,971
[SPEAKER_01]: I need something more specific to anchor my mind because I'm too busy, too distracted.

295
00:30:44,172 --> 00:30:47,895
[SPEAKER_01]: It might work for one or two minutes and then I'm just spaced out for the next two hours.

296
00:30:48,495 --> 00:30:56,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Would it not be better to introduce some object in front of me that I can fiddle around with to anchor my mind in that direction first?

297
00:30:56,682 --> 00:30:56,882
[SPEAKER_03]: Right.

298
00:30:57,202 --> 00:31:01,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is a very common idea, you know, taught on meditation retreats.

299
00:31:01,546 --> 00:31:07,673
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, there's this presumed gradual development of mindfulness there.

300
00:31:08,834 --> 00:31:14,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But, there are some risks connected with that, because you are already moving away from the context.

301
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:18,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody finds themselves sort of in between these two domains,

302
00:31:19,473 --> 00:31:32,997
[SPEAKER_01]: What can they practically do to strengthen that context and that understanding without falling back again in the old groove of wanting to be with something in front of you because you think that is how your mind calms down?

303
00:31:33,977 --> 00:31:36,058
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what do you think?

304
00:31:37,798 --> 00:31:43,660
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think it's a general thing that we take the Buddha's teaching quite the other way around.

305
00:31:44,192 --> 00:32:10,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, we start with, like, meditation, sitting, and then we go, oh, maybe I should maybe moderate my eating, maybe my sleep as well, or what my intention is actually, but still, I'm sitting there, and then we come to the precept, maybe one day, and I think if one person find himself, like, that this context, this mindfulness is too spacious for him, it's just because he's doing actively things that prevent him to discern that mindfulness that is already there.

306
00:32:10,559 --> 00:32:12,740
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the thing is, like,

307
00:32:16,219 --> 00:32:28,405
[SPEAKER_02]: This person should realize that it's not so much about doing certain things actively right now, but it's restraining myself for doing things that prevent me to discern what mindfulness is, what we are talking about.

308
00:32:28,425 --> 00:32:32,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So what does somebody, more practically perhaps, need to restrain?

309
00:32:33,787 --> 00:32:39,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this context, as you actually said in the beginning, it's not that you magically lose it.

310
00:32:39,891 --> 00:32:47,835
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense, it is because one makes certain choices, that that context, that one falls away, so to speak, from the context.

311
00:32:48,395 --> 00:32:50,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does one prevent that from happening?

312
00:32:50,697 --> 00:32:56,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is how you can have the openness and the spaciousness, without falling into the absorption of the other direction.

313
00:32:56,960 --> 00:32:59,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, not to mystify openness and spaciousness.

314
00:32:59,742 --> 00:33:02,003
[SPEAKER_03]: Again, it's like... it's a...

315
00:33:03,543 --> 00:33:07,325
[SPEAKER_03]: It's something that you already have some experience of.

316
00:33:07,365 --> 00:33:09,966
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just that the theme of it is not the right one.

317
00:33:10,106 --> 00:33:11,346
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just mundane things.

318
00:33:16,668 --> 00:33:26,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would a person then practically do to sustain that context?

319
00:33:29,732 --> 00:33:40,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it depends where this person is, but let's say, like, someone that never heard anything and start right now, it will be, like, respecting the precept and then sensory strength and then so on, like, kind of the gradual training.

320
00:33:41,005 --> 00:33:42,947
[SPEAKER_02]: And... yeah.

321
00:33:43,647 --> 00:33:48,693
[SPEAKER_02]: And also, like, if this person, like, something that I kind of hear a lot as well is, like,

322
00:33:49,208 --> 00:33:50,989
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, I need these people to meditate.

323
00:33:51,069 --> 00:33:53,191
[SPEAKER_02]: I need this group meditation and stuff like that.

324
00:33:53,251 --> 00:33:56,453
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's also, like, kind of something that someone should consider.

325
00:33:56,473 --> 00:34:02,517
[SPEAKER_02]: That if he's looking for someone else to be there in order to meditate, that is because he's looking for a reward again.

326
00:34:02,818 --> 00:34:08,161
[SPEAKER_02]: He's hoping for something to... to an experience to arise.

327
00:34:08,361 --> 00:34:08,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

328
00:34:09,222 --> 00:34:14,145
[SPEAKER_03]: And also, can you keep the precepts if you're not mindful?

329
00:34:15,365 --> 00:34:17,667
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the whole point.

330
00:34:17,727 --> 00:34:39,525
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that is actually... when it feels like these four satipaṭṭhānas are too difficult, because actually there's a sutta where the Buddha takes even monks, actually, not lay people, and he instructs them in five things that are basic for their training, and none of those five things are the four satipaṭṭhānas.

331
00:34:41,027 --> 00:34:50,155
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the precepts, sense-restraint, moderation in eating, restraining their speech, and developing right views.

332
00:34:50,695 --> 00:34:54,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I think it says there, developing seclusion.

333
00:34:57,500 --> 00:34:59,842
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the instruction for newly going forth.

334
00:35:00,002 --> 00:35:00,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

335
00:35:00,882 --> 00:35:04,384
[SPEAKER_01]: But so... Yeah, so, it's along those same lines.

336
00:35:04,584 --> 00:35:06,205
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say develop satipaṭṭhāna.

337
00:35:06,265 --> 00:35:06,486
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

338
00:35:06,566 --> 00:35:13,450
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because satipaṭṭhāna, if you look at the gradual training, that comes already when the person is in the position to abandon the five hindrances.

339
00:35:13,890 --> 00:35:22,435
