[SPEAKER_01]: So that is actually the one-pointedness, quote-unquote.

515
00:49:59,263 --> 00:50:03,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It talks about the totality of experience that doesn't allow any room for something to be outside of it.

516
00:50:04,847 --> 00:50:09,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is a different kind of mindfulness than focusing on one thing to the exclusion of everything else.

517
00:50:09,649 --> 00:50:15,831
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how samādhi would help you to see patisampada, which is the simultaneous dependence of two things.

518
00:50:16,091 --> 00:50:27,455
[SPEAKER_03]: You would be able to see then that the point of view or the setup that occurs when you're absorbed is exactly what won't allow you to actually see that simultaneous dependence.

519
00:50:27,495 --> 00:50:32,497
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why people who practice absorption think patisampada is like seeing a sequence

520
00:50:33,317 --> 00:50:35,398
[SPEAKER_03]: Of factors arising.

521
00:50:35,939 --> 00:50:37,960
[SPEAKER_03]: But the dhamma is timeless, as the Buddha said.

522
00:50:37,980 --> 00:50:54,011
[SPEAKER_03]: So, having that setup of two things enduring at the same time, the context and what you're directly attendant to, you start to realize that none of those two directions can fundamentally be taken as yours.

523
00:50:55,091 --> 00:50:57,073
[SPEAKER_03]: In the sense of, like, you can choose to call it yours.

524
00:50:57,973 --> 00:51:03,597
[SPEAKER_03]: But, eh... how can it really be yours if it depends on something else?

525
00:51:04,017 --> 00:51:04,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

526
00:51:04,357 --> 00:51:14,744
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was just gonna ask that, like, why is it that rightly discerning this principle of dependent origination is going to uproot or efface that sense of self?

527
00:51:26,329 --> 00:51:28,330
[SPEAKER_00]: This is kind of what I was trying to say earlier.

528
00:51:28,350 --> 00:51:38,237
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have kind of both aspects, you have perspective on both, then you're not either.

529
00:51:39,337 --> 00:51:39,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

530
00:51:40,278 --> 00:51:42,159
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be... if you are one,

531
00:51:43,752 --> 00:51:47,814
[SPEAKER_01]: But you see that that one depends on something else, which are not that one that got it.

532
00:51:47,854 --> 00:51:50,455
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're the other one, it's the same thing.

533
00:51:50,615 --> 00:51:54,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get minced in between, basically.

534
00:51:54,077 --> 00:52:03,401
[SPEAKER_03]: The thing is, when there isn't that mindfulness established on that right level, even if you choose to say, this is not mine,

535
00:52:04,677 --> 00:52:10,221
[SPEAKER_03]: What you're actually taking is yours, is that sort of point of view, that observer.

536
00:52:10,521 --> 00:52:10,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

537
00:52:11,742 --> 00:52:15,765
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, whether you realize it or not, it's not even like a matter of choice.

538
00:52:17,186 --> 00:52:21,829
[SPEAKER_03]: But that mindfulness, precisely, is supposed to come before that observer.

539
00:52:22,149 --> 00:52:23,050
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the whole point of it.

540
00:52:24,231 --> 00:52:29,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Like the observer, what's explored by the mind, you would start to see, even that thing that I regarded in myself,

541
00:52:31,052 --> 00:52:37,836
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you have expanded your perspective, this vantage point now allows you to see, wait, that's actually just another phenomenon.

542
00:52:38,736 --> 00:52:47,301
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas before, that phenomenon was still a phenomenon, but because you were focused on what was over there, it seemed like it was more than just a phenomenon.

543
00:52:47,361 --> 00:52:49,142
[SPEAKER_03]: This one is more special than the others.

544
00:52:49,162 --> 00:52:49,842
[SPEAKER_03]: That sense of self.

545
00:52:49,962 --> 00:52:50,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

546
00:52:51,343 --> 00:52:57,286
[SPEAKER_02]: Can we say that as long as someone tries to make the effort to look something as not mine,

547
00:52:59,505 --> 00:53:04,268
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, like, seeing things as not mine should not... it's a recognition that doesn't take effort.

548
00:53:04,408 --> 00:53:06,689
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not something that is actively done.

549
00:53:06,870 --> 00:53:13,834
[SPEAKER_03]: And the five aggregates, which are the main things that you have to understand, are not yours.

550
00:53:14,574 --> 00:53:18,296
[SPEAKER_03]: Five aggregates are never what you're looking at, or at least that's not the aspect that matters.

551
00:53:19,097 --> 00:53:21,498
[SPEAKER_03]: The aspect of the five aggregates is that

552
00:53:22,719 --> 00:53:24,800
[SPEAKER_03]: background aspect of it that's always there.

553
00:53:24,860 --> 00:53:35,507
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the feeling that's enduring while you're focused on this, the perception that's enduring, the ideas, the notions that are enduring, consciousness fundamentally, is always that background.

554
00:53:36,247 --> 00:53:41,431
[SPEAKER_03]: And, if you think you're watching your object, your consciousness as an object, which people would sometimes think,

555
00:53:42,351 --> 00:53:43,712
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not consciousness anymore.

556
00:53:43,832 --> 00:53:44,032
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

557
00:53:44,272 --> 00:53:46,973
[SPEAKER_03]: Consciousness is what's doing the watching.

558
00:53:47,733 --> 00:53:56,917
[SPEAKER_03]: And, like, sometimes there's a view, okay, when you have developed your focusing skills, then you can turn your attention to see consciousness itself.

559
00:53:57,637 --> 00:54:03,620
[SPEAKER_03]: But you can't see consciousness itself, first of all, and second of all, it's not later that you're supposed to do that.

560
00:54:03,820 --> 00:54:03,900
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

561
00:54:03,920 --> 00:54:07,422
[SPEAKER_03]: The practice starts with putting the point of view in perspective.

562
00:54:07,802 --> 00:54:08,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

563
00:54:08,582 --> 00:54:14,985
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to say, oh, let me focus on this for a little while, to a step, and then I'll switch it over, that's already missing the point.

564
00:54:15,206 --> 00:54:15,346
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

565
00:54:15,406 --> 00:54:16,606
[SPEAKER_03]: You start by switching it over.

566
00:54:16,646 --> 00:54:18,667
[SPEAKER_03]: That would have been the right mindfulness.

567
00:54:19,028 --> 00:54:24,110
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, if you go in the direction of concentration, by that time, like, your mind is already constricted.

568
00:54:24,370 --> 00:54:32,735
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, anything you look at, even if you have increased powers of observation, all of it is coming through that directionality of me looking at it.

569
00:54:33,352 --> 00:54:33,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

570
00:54:34,373 --> 00:54:35,194
[SPEAKER_03]: Even more than before.

571
00:54:35,354 --> 00:54:39,239
[SPEAKER_01]: We actually want to see that that attention is basically also there on its own.

572
00:54:39,379 --> 00:54:39,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

573
00:54:39,659 --> 00:54:42,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not you attending fundamentally.

574
00:54:42,102 --> 00:54:46,227
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why as well, maybe the last thing we can clear up is

575
00:54:48,220 --> 00:54:53,962
[SPEAKER_03]: There can be no mindfulness by itself, like, in the context of the path.

576
00:54:54,362 --> 00:54:58,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Because usually, it's like, okay, I start with mindfulness and that will take me to right view.

577
00:54:59,604 --> 00:55:00,584
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's the other way around.

578
00:55:00,864 --> 00:55:06,826
[SPEAKER_03]: It's knowing what you're supposed to recollect, knowing how you're supposed to recollect, knowing what the context is.

579
00:55:07,727 --> 00:55:10,988
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a... that is what allows you to be properly mindful.

580
00:55:12,088 --> 00:55:14,429
[SPEAKER_03]: So, actually, it's more of like...

581
00:55:16,659 --> 00:55:18,000
[SPEAKER_03]: Instead of...

582
00:55:18,040 --> 00:55:19,981
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, I have my idea of mindfulness.

583
00:55:20,221 --> 00:55:25,105
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me start doing it now, so I can get the result of stream entry, or whatever you're aiming for.

584
00:55:26,205 --> 00:55:32,649
[SPEAKER_03]: It should be by understanding what mindfulness is to begin with, I will be able to reach stream entry or whatnot.

585
00:55:32,669 --> 00:55:43,837
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like... Let's say, like, your mindfulness practice is like your technique or your form with which you do a certain physical exercise.

586
00:55:45,088 --> 00:55:51,253
[SPEAKER_03]: And if it's just the first day, you see somebody else grabbing the weight, and you go and do the same.

587
00:55:51,614 --> 00:55:54,256
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, now, I'm just going to do this until I get strong.

588
00:55:54,876 --> 00:55:59,060
[SPEAKER_03]: And you never actually took the time to perfect your form.

589
00:55:59,560 --> 00:56:00,941
[SPEAKER_03]: How you actually do the exercise?

590
00:56:01,682 --> 00:56:04,584
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you're not even doing it properly.

591
00:56:04,784 --> 00:56:07,567
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the... that would be the...

592
00:56:07,587 --> 00:56:08,848
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is going to backfire at some point.

593
00:56:08,868 --> 00:56:09,188
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

594
00:56:09,268 --> 00:56:10,929
[SPEAKER_03]: So that would be the point with...

595
00:56:13,011 --> 00:56:22,724
[SPEAKER_03]: The whole practice, it's like, first of all, in order to gain the right view, your goal should be learn how to do the exercise properly to begin with.

596
00:56:23,305 --> 00:56:24,586
[SPEAKER_03]: But see, the thing is that's boring.

597
00:56:25,685 --> 00:56:34,189
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not as fun as, you know, grabbing the five five-pound dumbbell and really focusing on, like, okay, what's my shoulder positioning?

598
00:56:34,609 --> 00:56:40,011
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I... and then, okay, I'm just gonna grab 50 pounds and then I'm just gonna cheat however way I want.

599
00:56:40,171 --> 00:56:41,152
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

600
00:56:41,392 --> 00:56:45,173
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the... that's why people don't want to do it.

601
00:56:45,453 --> 00:56:47,774
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, well, it's a form of impatience, basically.

602
00:56:47,874 --> 00:56:50,976
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's... it ends up backfiring, as you said.

603
00:56:51,096 --> 00:56:52,576
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

604
00:56:52,656 --> 00:56:54,077
[SPEAKER_02]: It's impatience and also, like,

605
00:56:55,348 --> 00:57:01,430
[SPEAKER_02]: People want to do the practice in their own way, the way they like it, the way they... kind of, it feels good, it's nice.

606
00:57:01,730 --> 00:57:09,833
[SPEAKER_02]: It's kind of like I heard, like, one time I was reading a book, and there was this teacher saying, like, oh, yeah, you just take from, like, all the tradition and you make your own pizza.

607
00:57:10,413 --> 00:57:13,715
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, if you never learn to bake a pizza, that's gonna taste terrible.

608
00:57:13,755 --> 00:57:19,217
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you're gonna put, like, fish and then whatever else on top, and there's just never gonna be a pizza, basically.

609
00:57:20,436 --> 00:57:34,321
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so you actually need to develop your own criteria as to what's right and wrong before you can practice, because there is no guarantee otherwise that you... Yeah, but those criteria obviously have to come from the actual right instruction.

610
00:57:34,361 --> 00:57:34,881
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

611
00:57:35,081 --> 00:57:41,303
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it has to be a combination of both, but it's always that... well, that's another context you can develop.

612
00:57:41,363 --> 00:57:43,103
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, I don't understand.

613
00:57:44,464 --> 00:57:47,505
[SPEAKER_03]: That's a mindfulness that you keep, and if you lose that,

614
00:57:49,007 --> 00:58:02,794
[SPEAKER_03]: You will start then taking for granted things that feel good to you, or seems like it helps, or ignoring the fact that, as the Buddha said, we are enveloped within ignorance, like within an egg.

615
00:58:03,474 --> 00:58:09,297
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't see... you don't see the way out, even if it seems the way out, like the way out for you.

616
00:58:10,640 --> 00:58:13,002
[SPEAKER_03]: Until you're at least a sotāpanna, it's not the way out.

617
00:58:13,262 --> 00:58:23,330
[SPEAKER_03]: It's some form of improving what you feel, but it's not a form of actual disowning, actual ending of craving.

618
00:58:24,331 --> 00:58:25,132
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's...

619
00:58:28,674 --> 00:58:33,595
[SPEAKER_03]: That's one of the mindfulnesses that a person should put first.

620
00:58:33,695 --> 00:58:36,816
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why faith, as well, is the first of the faculties.

621
00:58:36,936 --> 00:58:39,437
[SPEAKER_03]: Mindfulness is actually not the first of the five faculties.

622
00:58:39,497 --> 00:58:39,857
[SPEAKER_03]: It's faith.

623
00:58:40,517 --> 00:58:55,082
[SPEAKER_03]: And faith is basically that recognition that I'm always willing to give up what I think is a practice if I come across the instruction of wherever I put faith and saying that that's wrong.

624
00:58:55,422 --> 00:58:56,102
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what faith is.

625
00:58:56,700 --> 00:58:56,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

